.TH FAUCHE 1 "2026-08-01" "gardien-garde" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
fauche \- clear a repository off this host once every byte of it is recoverable elsewhere
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" bashify: norun
.B fauche list
[\fIrepo\fR...]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B fauche check
\fIrepo\fR...
.br
.B fauche script
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fauche
decides whether a repository on this host is expendable, on one condition:
every byte of it is recoverable from somewhere that is not this host. It
never deletes anything itself.
.B fauche script
writes a removal script to standard output for a human to read and run, so
the deletion is that human's act, in that human's own shell, at a moment
that human chose. A utility that both judges a repository expendable and
destroys it has one failure mode with no survivors.
.PP
The condition is recoverability, never tidiness. A repository is removable
only when every local branch is pushed to
.BR origin ,
the working tree holds no uncommitted or untracked file, no registered
worktree outside the repository depends on its object store, and every prose
file in it has a counterpart in the vault. A repository that fails any of
these is kept, with each failing reason named on its own line.
.PP
A check that cannot read what it needs counts as a reason to keep, never as
a reason to remove. An unreadable vault means the state of the prose is
unknown, and an unknown is reported as an unknown: a removal resting on a
blank is the outcome this utility exists to prevent.
.TP
.B list
Report every repository under the projects directory as
.B REMOVABLE
or
.BR KEEP ,
each kept one carrying its reasons. Named repositories restrict the report
to those. This is the default: run with no subcommand,
.B fauche
does exactly this.
.TP
.B check
Report the same verdict for the named repositories, exiting 5 if any of them
is kept. This is the form a script asks.
.TP
.B script
Write a removal script covering every repository found removable. The script
is commentary and
.BR "rm \-rf" ,
one repository per stanza, each stanza carrying the
.B git clone
command that recovers it. Repositories that are kept are named on standard
error instead, so the script on standard output is a file a reader can read
whole.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Report the same findings as machine\-readable output.
.TP
.B \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Report counts only, without the per\-repository commentary.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Report this utility's own surface: its subcommands, its flags, and which of
them can spend.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Report the version of this utility.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The repositories asked about were judged, and the answer stands.
.TP
.B 2
Usage: an unknown subcommand, an unknown flag, or a subcommand given no
repository where it requires one.
.TP
.B 5
At least one named repository is kept: it is not recoverable elsewhere, so
clearing it off this host loses something.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask about a path that is not there:
.PP
.nf
$ fauche check /nonexistent
KEEP          /nonexistent
  - no such directory
$ echo $?
5
.fi
.PP
Ask for something this utility does not offer:
.PP
.nf
$ fauche graze
fauche: unknown subcommand: graze  (try: fauche list)
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.PP
Ask which version is installed:
.PP
.nf
$ fauche --version
fauche (bashified)
$ echo $?
0
.fi
.SH COST
.B fauche
reads git metadata and files on this machine. It does not spend money, and
therefore carries no
.B summon
flag: not on
.BR list ,
not on
.BR check ,
not on
.BR script .
No subcommand on this page can spend. The shared runtime rejects the summon
flag here with a usage error rather than ignoring it, so the boundary is
stated by the tool as well as by this page. There is deliberately no short
form of that flag anywhere in this family: spelling the word out is the
deliberateness.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I CONTRACT.md
The promise this utility keeps.
.TP
.I GAPS.md
What the contract names and the tooling does not cover.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR rsync (1)
